ned almost obsessively to gaining domination over time. This urge is substantiated in their ritual of conversing with their dead ancestors: year after year, tribals in many parts of India worship terracotta or carved-wood objects representing their ancestors, aspiring to enter a trance in which they can converse with the dead. Over the centuries, an amazingly sharp memory has helped tribals classify material and natural objects into a highly complex system of knowledge. . . One of the main characteristics of the tribal arts is their distinct manner of constructing space and imagery, which might be described as ''hallucinatory''. In both oral and visual forms of representation, tribal artists seem to interpret verbal or pictorial space as demarcated by an extremely flexible ''frame''. The boundaries between art and non-art become almost invisible. A tribal epic can begin its narration from a trivial everyday event; tribal paintings merge with living space as if the two were one and the same. And within the narrative itself, or within the painted imagery, there is no deliberate attempt to follow a sequence. The episodes retold and the images created take on the apparently chaotic shapes of dreams. In a way, the syntax of language and the grammar of painting are the same, as if literature were painted words and painting were a song of images.', options = '["For tribals, conversing with the dead becomes a way of seeking control over time.", "Tribals show respect to their ancestors through terracotta and carved-wood objects.", "Tribals possess a sophisticated knowledge system that is based on memory.", "Tribals seek territorial domination over the spaces that they inhabit."]'::jsonb, explanation = 'Official CAT 2025 Slot 3, VARC Q4. Answer key cross-checked against https://online.2iim.com/CAT-question-paper/CAT-2025-Question-Paper-Slot-3-VARC/', has_figure = FALSE WHERE id = 'cat-2025-s3-varc-q04';
